## 1) Severity: MEDIUM | Type: Performance
Title: Wrap app imports in thunks so the app store actually loads lazily

Affected:
- packages/app-store/index.ts:1-31

Explanation:
Every `import("./x")` is a call that runs while the object literal is being built. All 29 app packages therefore start loading as soon as `@calcom/app-store` is imported. That is the same eager cost as the old static imports, so the stated goal of async loading is not met. It also makes things worse in two ways:

- Each package is now split into its own async chunk, which adds bundling overhead.
- If any app module fails to load (missing dependency, a throw at module evaluation), the rejected promise sits in the map. Until some code path happens to `await` that key, it is an unhandled rejection. In Node with default settings, that can crash the process even when the failing app is never used.

Proposed fix:
Store factory functions and call them on demand. Bundlers and Node cache the module, so repeated calls are cheap.

~~~ts
const appStore = {
  applecalendar: () => import("./applecalendar"),
  caldavcalendar: () => import("./caldavcalendar"),
  // ...
};
export default appStore;

// usage
const loader = appStore[key as keyof typeof appStore];
const app = loader ? await loader() : undefined;
~~~

## 2) Severity: MEDIUM | Type: Bug
Title: Stop using `forEach(async …)`, which escapes the try/catch and leaves promises dangling

Affected:
- packages/app-store/vital/lib/reschedule.ts:125-131
- packages/app-store/wipemycalother/lib/reschedule.ts:125-131
- packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings.tsx:553-561

Explanation:
`forEach` ignores the promises its async callbacks return. Two consequences follow:

- **Errors are not caught.** Anything thrown by `await getCalendar(...)`, `deleteEvent`, or `deleteMeeting` becomes an unhandled rejection. The surrounding `try { ... } catch` in the reschedule helpers no longer sees these errors, although it used to catch synchronous `getCalendar` failures.
- **Nothing waits for the deletions.** The handler continues (emails, DB updates, response) before the calendar and video deletions have even started. This can produce race conditions and silently drop failures.

Proposed fix:
~~~ts
await Promise.all(
  bookingRefsFiltered.map(async (bookingRef) => {
    if (!bookingRef.uid) return;
    if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_calendar")) {
      const calendar = await getCalendar(credentialsMap.get(bookingRef.type));
      return calendar?.deleteEvent(bookingRef.uid, builder.calendarEvent);
    } else if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_video")) {
      return deleteMeeting(credentialsMap.get(bookingRef.type), bookingRef.uid);
    }
  })
);
~~~
Alternatively, use `Promise.allSettled` if one failure should not block the others.

## 3) Severity: LOW | Type: Bug
Title: `getCalendarCredentials` now returns a Promise in `calendar`, which may break other callers

Affected:
- packages/core/CalendarManager.ts:28-32

Explanation:
`getCalendar` is now async, so the `calendar` field returned by `getCalendarCredentials` is a `Promise<Calendar | null>`. `getConnectedCalendars` was updated to `await item.calendar`. However, any other consumer of `getCalendarCredentials` (outside this diff) that checks `if (calendar)` or calls `calendar.method()` will now always see a truthy Promise and fail at runtime.

There is a second problem: these promises are created eagerly. If one rejects and is never awaited, it becomes an unhandled rejection.

I can't see all callers from this diff, so the severity is lowered.

Proposed fix:
Either make `getCalendarCredentials` async and resolve the calendars inside it, or rename the field (e.g. `calendarPromise`) so every caller is forced to `await` it.

~~~ts
export const getCalendarCredentials = async (credentials: Array<CredentialPayload>) => {
  // ...
  const calendar = await getCalendar(credential);
  // ...
};
~~~
